    "id": "train-1951",
    "question": "Marnie opens a bag of chips and eats 5 of them to see if she likes them. She does, so she eats 5 more. The bag has 100 chips in it and starting on the second day she has them, Marnie eats 10 each day. How many days does it take for Marnie to eat the whole bag of chips?",
    "gold": "10",
    "answer": "Marnie has already eaten 5 of the chips + 5 more = <<5+5=10>>10 chips Marnie ate.\nThe bag has 100 chips in it and Marnie has only eaten 10, 100 - 10 = <<100-10=90>>90 chips left.\nIf Marnie eats 10 chips a day starting from the second day she has them, it will take her 90 chips in the bag / 10 chips Marine eats a day = <<90/10=9>>9 days to eat the whole bag.\nHowever, Marnie also ate chips on the first day she bought them, so 9 + 1 = <<9+1=10>>10 days to eat the whole bag.\n#### 10"
  },
  "generation": {
    "text": "To determine how many days it takes for Marnie to eat the whole bag of chips, we need to break down the problem step by step.\n\n1. **Initial Consumption:**\n   Marnie eats 5 chips on the first day and then eats 5 more chips on the second day. So, in the first two days, she eats:\n   \\[\n   5 + 5 = 10 \\text{ chips}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Remaining Chips:**\n   The bag originally has 100 chips, and after the first two days, she has eaten 10 chips. Therefore, the number of chips left is:\n   \\[\n   100 - 10 = 90 \\text{ chips}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Daily Consumption:**\n   Starting from the third day, Marnie eats 10 chips each day. We need to find out how many days it takes for her to eat the remaining 90 chips. Since she eats 10 chips per day, the number of days required to eat 90 chips is:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{90}{10} = 9 \\text{ days}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Total Days:**\n   Adding the first two days to the 9 days from the third day, the total number of days it takes for Marnie to eat the whole bag of chips is:\n   \\[\n   2 + 9 = 11 \\text{ days}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{11}\n\\]",
